01:09.120 --> 01:15.880
repeated loss of previously performed cognitive labor. Exactly. Like think about the mechanics of

01:15.880 --> 01:21.820
a normal chronological feed. Somebody else already asked that basic FAQ last week, right? And the thinker

01:21.820 --> 01:26.740
already took the time to answer it. Yes. A different person already submitted that repetitive

01:26.740 --> 01:31.600
debunked argument and it was already refuted. But because our communication systems are just flat

01:31.600 --> 01:37.500
time ordered cues, the recipient is basically forced to start from zero every single time they open a new

01:37.500 --> 01:43.240
message. It's wildly inefficient. We are operating on a paradigm that basically treats human attention as

01:43.240 --> 01:49.320
an infinite receptacle. Right. Rather than a rigorously protected, strictly limited computational resource,

01:49.320 --> 01:55.460
which is why today's deep dive is so fascinating. To address this many to one bottleneck, we are looking

01:55.460 --> 02:01.840
at two massive revolutionary texts published back in August, 2026 by Flyxion, independent researcher.

